course: dictionary
lesson: 8963
title: Simulation: a safe copy of the real thing
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A simulation is a copy of a real situation, made on a computer or with models, so that you can study it or practise it safely. «A simulation is a model of something real, built to explore or learn safely.» The clue is the word safely: a simulation lets you learn without any real danger.
explain: «A flight simulator teaches pilots without crashing real planes.» Ask yourself what word would fit here. A pilot could crash a real plane, which is dangerous, so a flight simulator must be a pretend version that is safe to use. Swap in your meaning: "a safe copy of a plane" still makes sense, so you have it right.
explain: Scientists and computing pupils use simulations to see things that are too big, small, fast, slow or dangerous to test for real, like planets moving or volcanoes. «Your lessons are a simulation, and your learning in them is real learning.» So a simulation can be pretend, but what you learn from it can still be real.
fact: A simulation is a copy of a real situation, made on a computer or with models.
fact: Common phrases are «computer simulation», «flight simulator» and «run a simulation».
fact: In maths, a simulation can repeat a random event, like rolling a dice many times on a computer, to estimate a probability.
fact: Simulation can also mean pretending, as in the formal phrase «a simulation of interest», which is a false show.
fact: The simulation idea was made famous by the philosopher Nick Bostrom in 2003, but no one has evidence that we live in one.
exercise: «A flight simulator teaches pilots without crashing real planes.» Why do pilots use a flight simulator? | To sell more plane tickets | To watch films while they fly | *To practise safely, without real danger | To make the real plane go faster
exercise: In maths, a computer simulation can roll a dice many times. What is this used to estimate? | A colour of paint | A recipe for soup | The orbit of a planet | *A probability
exercise: Which of these is a simulation? | A letter to a friend | *A computer model of a volcano erupting | A painting of a sunny field | A shopping list
test: «Your lessons are a simulation, and your learning in them is real learning.» What does this sentence suggest about simulations? | They are real planets | They are a kind of food | *They can be pretend, yet still teach real things | They are always easy to see
test: In the phrase «a simulation of interest», what does simulation mean? | A very fast computer | *Pretending, putting on a false show | A safe place to swim | A copy of a planet
exam: Why do scientists use simulations? | *To study things that are too big, small, fast, slow or dangerous to test for real | To stop all experiments from ever happening | To make everything in the world cheaper to buy | To prove that the world is a computer
